PHASE CONJUGATE WAVE REFLECTION
Time-reversed wave propagation

MAP
  ψ  ↦  ψ*
  Complex conjugation of monochromatic amplitude
  ≡ time reversal  t  →  -t

FIELD
  Re[ ψ(r) exp(-i ω t) ]
  After conjugation of complex amplitude
  becomes the field that would exist if t itself
  had been sent to -t.

CONSEQUENCE
  Distortion collected on the outward path
  is cancelled on the return.
  The wave retraces its own history.

HISTORY
  Zel’dovich (1972)
  Stimulated Brillouin scattering
  returned an aberrated laser beam
  as its own undistorted predecessor.

MIRROR TYPES
  Ordinary mirror     : reflects
  Phase-conjugate     : restores

VARIABLES
  ψ          complex wave amplitude
  ψ*         complex conjugate
  r          position
  t          time
  ω          angular frequency
  k          wave number
  λ          wavelength
  n          refractive index
  d1, d2     distances to field point
  M1, M2     interacting masses
  ε0         permittivity of free space
  Dx, Dy, Dz electric flux density components
  nx, ny, nz refractive-index components

FUNDAMENTALS
  Fresnel zone
    R_Fv = √[ n λ (d1 + d2) ]

  Field equation
    F_n = -k² T_μν

  Energy density
    u = ½ ( Dx²/(ε0 nx²) + Dy²/(ε0 ny²) + Dz²/(ε0 nz²) )
